"""MemDiver synthesized Volatility 3 plugin: memdiver_gocryptfs_synth (recipe v2).

Synthetic gocryptfs master key (recipe v2 test fixture)

Generated by memdiver.architect.vol3_recipe from the recipe embedded below as
RECIPE_JSON (schema memdiver-vol3-recipe/2). The plugin FINDS candidate keys
with the recipe's locators and PROVES each candidate with a format oracle whose
source is inlined verbatim (hash-checked) at the end of this file, so it runs
under stock Volatility 3 with no MemDiver install.

Proven column
-------------
``oracle:<kind>``  the inlined oracle verified the key against the sample
                   (a vault file, a header, ...): this IS the key.
``rejected``       the oracle (or a cheap prefilter: entropy, or the oracle's
                   own ``prefilter``) says it is not.
``unverified``     not decided: no oracle sample was available, the
                   ``--oracle-budget`` ran out (Detail ``budget: ...``), or the
                   oracle admits one key and it is already proven (Detail
                   ``skipped: key already proven``). A budget never rejects.
``error:<short>``  the oracle could not decide (missing backend, bad sample).

A weak anchor can only add candidates, never a false ``oracle:`` row: every
row's verdict comes from the oracle alone. Candidate counts are capped
(``max_hits`` / ``max_candidates`` in the recipe) and reported in the log.
By default only proven rows are printed (plus a few unproven ones when nothing
was proven); ``--show-unverified`` prints every candidate up to the row cap.

Scan target
-----------
* ``.msl`` process slice (layer metadata ``msl``): the stacked top layer,
  i.e. the captured process's virtual address space.
* ``--pid`` on a kernel image whose symbols resolve (``kernel`` module): each
  matching process's own layer (linux pslist), restricted to its VMAs (user
  space only), in process virtual addresses.
* otherwise: the PHYSICAL memory layer beneath the kernel/top layer (e.g. the
  LiME layer, so offsets are physical addresses and container headers are not
  scanned); a flat file with no memory layer is scanned as the file itself.

The ``kernel`` ModuleRequirement is declared FIRST and ``primary`` (which
``kernel.layer_name`` also satisfies) second: the LayerStacker fills the first
translation-layer requirement it finds, and only a filled
``kernel.layer_name`` lets the symbol automagic build a kernel module (with
``primary`` first, ``--pid`` could never be honoured).

Oracle sample
-------------
The sample embedded in the recipe proves keys of the artefact it was taken
from. ``--oracle-sample PATH`` replaces it: a packed sample blob, a raw
artefact file, or a directory the format knows how to read (for gocryptfs the
vault's cipher directory; the cipher is then read from its gocryptfs.conf).

Options: ``--exhaustive`` runs every locator even after the anchor proved a
key (to count candidates); ``--export`` writes a JSON report via the
framework's file handler (name: ``--export-path`` or ``<stem>.json``).
``--oracle-param key=value`` (repeatable) overrides an oracle parameter, e.g.
``cipher=aes-gcm`` when ``--oracle-sample`` is a raw content file without
its gocryptfs.conf. The entropy locator warns, with the covered fraction of
the layer, whenever its candidate cap stops it early.

Oracle budget
-------------
``--oracle-budget SECONDS`` (default 120, 0 = unlimited) bounds the time spent
in the oracle per run, NOT the scan. Candidates are verified anchor-first, then
in locator order, then by entropy (highest first); one log line estimates the
cost (candidates x the oracle's ``cost_us``, x20 on pure Python). Candidates
left when the budget runs out stay ``unverified`` with the estimate in Detail.
The oracle receives a ctx: the recipe's static ``oracle.ctx``, plus ``found``
(every candidate key of the pass), ``layer``, ``read(offset, length)`` (padded
read of the scanned layer), and per candidate ``offset`` and ``locators``.

Crypto backend
--------------
The inlined oracle proves keys with ``cryptography`` when it is installed,
else ``pycryptodome`` (shipped by ``volatility3[full]``), else the pure-Python
primitives inlined beside it, so a bare ``pip install volatility3`` proves
keys too. The choice is logged once: INFO for a library, one WARNING for pure
Python (correct, but slower). The Proven column means the same on every backend.
"""
